<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andreas Joseph Hofmann</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1752">1752</a>–<a href="/years/1849">1849</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andreas Joseph Hofmann was a German philosopher and professor at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> who supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>. In March 1793 he presided over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhenish-german-national-convention">Rhenish-German National Convention</a>, which proclaimed the Republic of Mainz, the first republic on German soil. After Prussian forces retook Mainz he lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and later returned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hofmann was born in Zell am Main near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a>, the son of a surgeon. Orphaned early, he was brought up by an uncle, probably the theologian Andreas Joseph Fahrmann, and studied law at the universities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> and Würzburg. In 1777 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> to gain experience at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aulic-council">Reichshofrat</a>, the Aulic Council, and became a Privatdozent in 1778. There he took up the ideas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephinism">Josephinism</a>, wrote on the history of philosophy and founded a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> journal in 1781, but his satirical articles brought conflict with the authorities and forced him to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1783.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1784 Elector Friedrich Karl von Erthal made him chair of philosophy at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, a centre of the Catholic Enlightenment; from 1791 he also held the chair of natural law. He had belonged to the Illuminati under the name Aulus Persius, and his students included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klemens-von-metternich">Klemens von Metternich</a> and Johann Adam von Itzstein. He welcomed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> and praised it in his lectures, and was under investigation when the archbishop's court fled before General Custine's troops, who entered Mainz on 21 October 1792.</p><p>Two days later he helped found the Mainz Jacobin club and became one of its leading orators, criticising both the old regime and the French military government. He published the pamphlet Aristokraten-Katechismus in late 1792 and campaigned in the countryside for the elections of February and March 1793. Elected president of the convention over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georg-forster">Georg Forster</a>, he proclaimed the Rhenish-German Free State from the balcony of the Deutschhaus on 18 March 1793 and three days later signed the decree asking for union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> he led a society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> republicans, fought royalist insurgents in the Vendée, and on a spying mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1794 was recognised by Metternich at a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-haydn">Joseph Haydn</a> concert. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-campo-formio">Treaty of Campo Formio</a> he became receveur général of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Mont-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonnerre">Tonnerre</a>, but resigned in 1803 when 750,000 francs went missing through a subordinate's fraud. He spent his last years in Winkel, watched by the authorities, and died on 6 September 1849, buried without a Catholic funeral. No portrait or papers of his survive.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Andreas Joseph Hofmann” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The convention, led by the professor <b>Andreas Joseph Hofmann</b> and the naturalist Georg Forster, abolished feudal rule.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-mainz">Republic of Mainz</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Zell am Main, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Winkel, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.82,9.87&amp;year=1815" aria-label="Zell am Main on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4776 430 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Andreas Joseph Hofmann"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5226 700h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5226 700h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.82,9.87&amp;year=1815">What was here in 1815? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
